[
    {
        "content": "<p>In joint work with Nate Ackerman and Robin Knight, we have formally verified a counterexample to Vaught’s conjecture for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_{\\omega_1,\\omega}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9694em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">ω</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3173em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.357em;margin-left:-0.0359em;margin-right:0.0714em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.5em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size3 size1 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.143em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mpunct mtight\">,</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">ω</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>, based on Knight’s unpublished manuscript.</p>\n<p>The formalization constructs a sentence in a countable relational language with exactly <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\aleph_1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8444em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">ℵ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> many countable models up to isomorphism and no perfect set of non-isomorphic models.</p>\n<p>This work combined Knight’s framework with extensive human-guided AI exploration and formalization, including revisions to the combinatorial construction and new mathematical ideas, using Claude Code and Codex with <a href=\"https://github.com/cameronfreer/lean4-skills\">lean4-skills</a> and <a href=\"https://github.com/oOo0oOo/lean-lsp-mcp\">lean-lsp-mcp</a>.</p>\n<p>The formal proof builds on mathlib and the <a href=\"https://github.com/cameronfreer/infinitary-logic\">infinitary-logic</a> library, into which we factored reusable machinery for infinitary syntax and semantics, back-and-forth equivalence, Scott analysis, and descriptive set theory.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://palomar-registry.org/entry?id=PALOMAR-2026-10-07-000001\">Comparator-checked proof on Palomar</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/cameronfreer/vaught-conjecture-counterexample/\">Streamlined Lean development</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://hexagonmath.org/2610.00188\">Initial paper outlining the proof strategy and many details</a>; a fuller account will follow.</li>\n</ul>",
        "id": 630753144,
        "sender_full_name": "Cameron Freer",
        "timestamp": 1791723153
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Congratulations! I have a question about the set theoretic content of the statement. Let's call the main result R. The work formalizes R, right? Not \"ZFC proves R\" which, in a sense, is the traditional framework in which the problem was posed. In most of mathematics this shouldn't matter but Vaught's conjecture is sitting closer to foundations more than most mathematics. In principle there are theorems of Lean which are independent of ZFC. Should we audit the proof with these in mind? By checking that, say, the universe level is below 1 everywhere or some other check for \\Pi^0_1 combinatorics? Or am i being paranoid here?</p>",
        "id": 632195403,
        "sender_full_name": "Sonat Suer",
        "timestamp": 1792139825
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks! We construct a counterexample to the \"absolute\" formulation of the infinitary Vaught conjecture, i.e., a sentence that has <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\aleph_1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8444em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">ℵ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> many countable models up to isomorphism and no perfect set of non-isomorphic models.</p>\n<p>This version of the statement is absolute between models of set theory, and in particular does not depend on whether the continuum hypothesis holds. This is implicit in Morley's 1970 paper <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2307/2271150\">\"The number of countable models\"</a>; for details see section 5 of Sacks's 2007 paper <a href=\"https://people.math.harvard.edu/~sacks/bws.pdf\">\"Bounds on weak scattering\"</a>.</p>\n<p>While this absoluteness on its own doesn't imply that the proof in Lean corresponds to one in ZFC, my sense is that there is no particular reason for additional foundational scrutiny beyond what would be appropriate for a typical mathematical result formalized in Lean. In particular, the set-theoretic concerns associated with the older formulation of Vaught's conjecture (stated in terms of having strictly between <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℵ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\aleph_0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8444em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">ℵ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℵ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{\\aleph_0}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">ℵ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3173em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.357em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.0714em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.5em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size3 size1 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.143em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> many countable models), don't arise here; that formulation is trivially satisfied under CH, whereas the absolute version of Vaught's conjecture discussed above is meaningful regardless of CH (and equivalent to this older one under <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">¬</span></span></span></span> CH, as shown by Morley).</p>\n<p>It would still be reasonable to check that the formalized proof can be interpreted in ZFC, though I don't know of anything in our argument that would suggest otherwise.</p>",
        "id": 632430631,
        "sender_full_name": "Cameron Freer",
        "timestamp": 1792202802
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thank you for the explanation. It seems I <em>was</em> being paranoid.</p>",
